This happened because the test is still enforcing airport-ident accuracy, even though the evaluation call is matching flights with `compare_airports=False`.

In [test_flights_algorithm.py](./tests/flights/evaluation/test_flights_algorithm.py:14), `airport_ident_match_pct` is in `BEST_STATS`, so the test requires it to stay above the old baseline:

```python
BEST_STATS = {
    "true_positive",
    "precision",
    "recall",
    "f1",
    "airport_ident_match_pct",
}
```

But `airport_ident_match_pct` is computed separately in [flights_comparison.py](./src/flights/flights_comparison.py:149) as exact airport-ident agreement among matched flights:

```python
airport_ident_match = df.filter(pl.col("same_airport_ident")).height
airport_ident_match_pct = airport_ident_match / true_positive
```

So F1/precision/recall can be fine while this fails.

Why it likely moved: your current algorithm now assigns airport idents from `segment.takeoff_message` / `segment.landing_message`, which are the first/last endpoint ADS-B rows. That can change the closest-airport guess versus the older event-time row, especially around dense airport areas, ramps, heliports, non-FAA idents, or nearby alternates. Also your current `flight_type.py` points output at `trad-model/v6`, so the test is reading a different cache/version than the baseline CSV.

Bottom line: this is not a flight-detection regression by itself. It is an airport-attribution metric regression, and the test is still treating that as a required bound. Since you said airport matching can be ignored, remove `airport_ident_match_pct` from `BEST_STATS` or split it into an informational stat.

